            "text": "At night, the city becomes an instrument nobody remembers learning to play. Pipes knock inside walls. A train draws its bow across the tracks. Somewhere, a refrigerator hums with the confidence of a creature that has never doubted its purpose. Even silence arrives carrying the faint electrical buzz of expectation.\n\nI like to imagine that lost things hold meetings during these hours. Gloves gather beneath benches. Keys compare the doors they once opened. The names we forget sit together politely, waiting for someone to recognize them. Nothing is truly gone, they insist, only temporarily employed by an entirely different story.\n\nMeanwhile, a baker measures flour into a bowl. Pale dust rises, then settles on the counter like weather. Bread is an ordinary miracle that wisely avoids advertising itself. Water, grain, warmth, patience. By morning, people will tear it apart and feel, briefly, that the world has kept a small promise.\n\nPerhaps this is what hope usually looks like: not a sunrise or a speech, but somebody preparing something useful before anyone asks. A clean cup. A repaired hinge. An extra blanket folded at the foot of a bed. Small gestures making quiet room for lives they cannot predict or control.\n\nBy dawn, the city will pretend it made itself. Cars will start. Screens will glow. Everyone will hurry toward whatever seems urgent. But underneath the noise, those unnoticed kindnesses will remain, holding the day together as invisibly as stitches inside a coat. We walk warmed by work we never see."
          }
